 objects themselves are not really meant to render out the headers or cells, but the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"header"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cell"},{"type":"text","value":" objects will contain references to these "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column"},{"type":"text","value":" objects from which they can derive the necessary information to render their UI."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const column = cell.column // get column from cell\nconst column = header.column // get column from header","lang":"js"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"column-table-instance-apis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Table Instance APIs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are dozens of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table"},{"type":"text","value":" instance APIs you can use to retrieve columns from the table instance. Which APIs you will use will depend entirely on which features you are using in your table and your use-case."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"get-column","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Get Column"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you need to just get a single column by its ID, you can use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.getColumn"},{"type":"text","value":" API."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const column = table.getColumn('firstName')","lang":"js"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"get-columns","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Get Columns"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The simplest column API is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.getAllColumns"},{"type":"text","value":", which will return a list of all columns in the table. There are dozens of other column APIs that are affected by other features and the state of the table that come alongside this API though. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.getAllFlatColumns"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.getAllLeafColumns"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.getCenterLeafColumns"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.getStartVisibleLeafColumns"},{"type":"text","value":" are just some examples of other column APIs that you might use in tandem with the column visibility or column pinning features."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"column-objects","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Objects"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column objects are not actually meant to be used to render out the table UI directly, so they are not associated 1-to-1 with any "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<th>"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<td>"},{"type":"text","value":" elements in your table, but they contain a lot of useful properties and methods that you can use to interact with the table state."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"column-ids","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column IDs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every column must have a unique "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"},{"type":"text","value":" defined in its associated "},{"type":"link","href":"./column-defs","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Definition"}]},{"type":"text","value":". Usually, you define this "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"},{"type":"text","value":" yourself, or it is derived from the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"accessorKey"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"header"},{"type":"text","value":" properties in the column definition."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"columndef","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ColumnDef"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A reference to the original "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnDef"},{"type":"text","value":" object that was used to create the column is always available on the column object."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"nested-grouped-columns-properties","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Nested Grouped Columns Properties"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are a few properties on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column"},{"type":"text","value":" objects that are only useful if the column is part of a nested or grouped column structure. These properties include:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columns"},{"type":"text","value":": An array of child columns that belong to a group column."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"depth"},{"type":"text","value":": The header group \"row index\" that the column group belongs to."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"parent"},{"type":"text","value":": The parent column of the column. If the column is a top-level column, this will be "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"more-column-apis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"More Column APIs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are dozens of Column APIs that you can use to interact with the table state and extract cell values from the table based on the state of the table. See each feature's column API documentation for more information."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"column-rendering","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Rendering"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Don't necessarily use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column"},{"type":"text","value":" objects to render "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"headers"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cells"},{"type":"text","value":" directly. Instead, use the "},{"type":"link","href":"./headers","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"header"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"link","href":"./cells","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cell"}]},{"type":"text","value":" objects, as discussed above."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"But if you are just rendering a list of columns somewhere else in your UI for something like a column visibility menu or something similar, you can just map over a columns array and render out the UI as you normally would."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"where-to-get-columns-from","text":"Where to Get Columns From","level":2},{"id":"header-and-cell-objects","text":"Header and Cell Objects","level":3},{"id":"column-table-instance-apis","text":"Column Table Instance APIs","level":3},{"id":"get-column","text":"Get Column","level":4},{"id":"get-columns","text":"Get Columns","level":4},{"id":"column-objects","text":"Column Objects","level":2},{"id":"column-ids","text":"Column IDs","level":3},{"id":"columndef","text":"ColumnDef","level":3},{"id":"nested-grouped-columns-properties","text":"Nested Grouped Columns Properties","level":3},{"id":"more-column-apis","text":"More Column APIs","level":2},{"id":"column-rendering","text":"Column Rendering","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n> [!NOTE]\n> This guide is about the actual `column` objects that are generated within the table instance and NOT about setting up the [column definitions](./column-defs) for your table.\n\nThis quick guide will discuss the different ways you can retrieve and interact with `column` objects in TanStack Table.\n\n## Where to Get Columns From\n\nYou can find the `column` objects in many places. They are often attached to other objects like headers and cells, and they are also available from several `table` instance APIs.\n\n### Header and Cell Objects\n\nBefore you reach for one of the `table` instance APIs, consider if you actually need to retrieve either [headers](./headers) or [cells](./cells) instead of `columns`. If you are rendering out the markup for your table, you will most likely want to reach for the APIs that return headers or cells instead of columns. The column objects themselves are not really meant to render out the headers or cells, but the `header` and `cell` objects will contain references to these `column` objects from which they can derive the necessary information to render their UI.\n\n```js\nconst column = cell.column // get column from cell\nconst column = header.column // get column from header\n```\n\n### Column Table Instance APIs\n\nThere are dozens of `table` instance APIs you can use to retrieve columns from the table instance. Which APIs you will use will depend entirely on which features you are using in your table and your use-case.\n\n#### Get Column\n\nIf you need to just get a single column by its ID, you can use the `table.getColumn` API.\n\n```js\nconst column = table.getColumn('firstName')\n```\n\n#### Get Columns\n\nThe simplest column API is `table.getAllColumns`, which will return a list of all columns in the table. There are dozens of other column APIs that are affected by other features and the state of the table that come alongside this API though. `table.getAllFlatColumns`, `table.getAllLeafColumns`, `table.getCenterLeafColumns`, `table.getStartVisibleLeafColumns` are just some examples of other column APIs that you might use in tandem with the column visibility or column pinning features.\n\n## Column Objects\n\nColumn objects are not actually meant to be used to render out the table UI directly, so they are not associated 1-to-1 with any `<th>` or `<td>` elements in your table, but they contain a lot of useful properties and methods that you can use to interact with the table state.\n\n### Column IDs\n\nEvery column must have a unique `id` defined in its associated [Column Definition](./column-defs). Usually, you define this `id` yourself, or it is derived from the `accessorKey` or `header` properties in the column definition.\n\n### ColumnDef\n\nA reference to the original `columnDef` object that was used to create the column is always available on the column object.\n\n### Nested Grouped Columns Properties\n\nThere are a few properties on `column` objects that are only useful if the column is part of a nested or grouped column structure. These properties include:\n\n- `columns`: An array of child columns that belong to a group column.\n- `depth`: The header group \"row index\" that the column group belongs to.\n- `parent`: The parent column of the column. If the column is a top-level column, this will be `undefined`.\n\n## More Column APIs\n\nThere are dozens of Column APIs that you can use to interact with the table state and extract cell values from the table based on the state of the table. See each feature's column API documentation for more information.\n\n## Column Rendering\n\nDon't necessarily use `column` objects to render `headers` or `cells` directly. Instead, use the [`header`](./headers) and [`cell`](./cells) objects, as discussed above.\n\nBut if you are just rendering a list of columns somewhere else in your UI for something like a column visibility menu or something similar, you can just map over a columns array and render out the UI as you normally would.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Columns Guide\n---\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> This guide is about the actual `column` objects that are generated within the table instance and NOT about setting up the [column definitions](./column-defs) for your table.\n\nThis quick guide will discuss the different ways you can retrieve and interact with `column` objects in TanStack Table.\n\n## Where to Get Columns From\n\nYou can find the `column` objects in many places. They are often attached to other objects like headers and cells, and they are also available from several `table` instance APIs.\n\n### Header and Cell Objects\n\nBefore you reach for one of the `table` instance APIs, consider if you actually need to retrieve either [headers](./headers) or [cells](./cells) instead of `columns`. If you are rendering out the markup for your table, you will most likely want to reach for the APIs that return headers or cells instead of columns. The column objects themselves are not really meant to render out the headers or cells, but the `header` and `cell` objects will contain references to these `column` objects from which they can derive the necessary information to render their UI.\n\n```js\nconst column = cell.column // get column from cell\nconst column = header.column // get column from header\n```\n\n### Column Table Instance APIs\n\nThere are dozens of `table` instance APIs you can use to retrieve columns from the table instance. Which APIs you will use will depend entirely on which features you are using in your table and your use-case.\n\n#### Get Column\n\nIf you need to just get a single column by its ID, you can use the `table.getColumn` API.\n\n```js\nconst column = table.getColumn('firstName')\n```\n\n#### Get Columns\n\nThe simplest column API is `table.getAllColumns`, which will return a list of all columns in the table. There are dozens of other column APIs that are affected by other features and the state of the table that come alongside this API though. `table.getAllFlatColumns`, `table.getAllLeafColumns`, `table.getCenterLeafColumns`, `table.getStartVisibleLeafColumns` are just some examples of other column APIs that you might use in tandem with the column visibility or column pinning features.\n\n## Column Objects\n\nColumn objects are not actually meant to be used to render out the table UI directly, so they are not associated 1-to-1 with any `<th>` or `<td>` elements in your table, but they contain a lot of useful properties and methods that you can use to interact with the table state.\n\n### Column IDs\n\nEvery column must have a unique `id` defined in its associated [Column Definition](./column-defs). Usually, you define this `id` yourself, or it is derived from the `accessorKey` or `header` properties in the column definition.\n\n### ColumnDef\n\nA reference to the original `columnDef` object that was used to create the column is always available on the column object.\n\n### Nested Grouped Columns Properties\n\nThere are a few properties on `column` objects that are only useful if the column is part of a nested or grouped column structure. These properties include:\n\n- `columns`: An array of child columns that belong to a group column.\n- `depth`: The header group \"row index\" that the column group belongs to.\n- `parent`: The parent column of the column. If the column is a top-level column, this will be `undefined`.\n\n## More Column APIs\n\nThere are dozens of Column APIs that you can use to interact with the table state and extract cell values from the table based on the state of the table. See each feature's column API documentation for more information.\n\n## Column Rendering\n\nDon't necessarily use `column` objects to render `headers` or `cells` directly. Instead, use the [`header`](./headers) and [`cell`](./cells) objects, as discussed above.\n\nBut if you are just rendering a list of columns somewhere else in your UI for something like a column visibility menu or something similar, you can just map over a columns array and render out the UI as you normally would.\n"}
